@page "/guides/reconnection"
@using BlazorBlueprint.Icons.Lucide.Components

<PageTitle>Reconnection - Blazor Blueprint</PageTitle>

<div class="max-w-4xl space-y-8">
    <div class="space-y-3">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Reconnection</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground">
            Paste one block into your host page and the Blazor Server reconnection dialog follows
            your theme instead of showing the framework's plain white box.
        </p>
    </div>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Try it</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            These buttons put the real dialog into each state the framework can set. Nothing is
            simulated — the markup and the CSS are the ones your application would ship.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            <BbButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => Preview("components-reconnect-show"))">Reconnecting</BbButton>
            <BbButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => Preview("components-reconnect-show components-reconnect-retrying"))">Counting down</BbButton>
            <BbButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => Preview("components-reconnect-paused"))">Paused</BbButton>
            <BbButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => Preview("components-reconnect-failed"))">Failed</BbButton>
            <BbButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => Preview("components-reconnect-rejected"))">Rejected</BbButton>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-r-lg border-s-4 border-primary bg-primary/5 p-4">
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
                Each preview clears itself after twelve seconds. The <strong class="text-foreground">Failed</strong>
                and <strong class="text-foreground">Rejected</strong> states carry working buttons —
                Reload really does reload.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Why the counter starts around 11</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            It is not skipping attempts. Blazor's default policy retries
            <strong class="text-foreground">ten times with no gap at all</strong>, then waits five
            seconds between attempts 11 and 20, then thirty seconds up to the limit of thirty. The
            first ten are over in a few hundred milliseconds — far faster than anyone can read — so
            the first number a person actually sees is 11.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            That is also why the countdown only appears from attempt 11. While the gap is zero there
            is nothing to count, and the framework only adds
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">components-reconnect-retrying</code>
            once there is a real wait. Change any of it with
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reconnectionOptions.retryIntervalMilliseconds</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">maxRetries</code> when you start Blazor.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Why it is markup and not a component</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The dialog appears when the circuit is down, so by definition nothing can render. Blazor
            drives it from static HTML in the host page: give the page an element with
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">id="components-reconnect-modal"</code>
            and the framework toggles state classes on it. With no such element it builds its own
            overlay and injects its own stylesheet, which is the unstyled box most Server
            applications ship with.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The styling is in <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">blazorblueprint.css</code>
            and reads your theme variables, so light and dark need no configuration.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Setup</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            Paste this into <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">App.razor</code>
            (or <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">index.html</code>) inside
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">&lt;body&gt;</code>. It is the same
            block this demo uses.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Source="Guides/Reconnection/markup.txt" />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">States</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The framework sets these classes on the modal element. An element carrying
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">data-when</code> shows only in the
            states it lists, so one block of markup serves all of them.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead class="bg-muted/50">
                    <tr>
                        <th class="p-3 text-start font-medium">Class</th>
                        <th class="p-3 text-start font-medium">When</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-show</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">Trying to reconnect.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-retrying</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">Added on top of <code class="text-xs">show</code> while counting down to the next attempt.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-paused</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">The circuit was paused and will resume on its own.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-hide</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">Reconnected. The dialog goes away.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-failed</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">Out of attempts. <code class="text-xs">Blazor.reconnect()</code> tries again.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-resume-failed</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">A paused circuit could not be resumed.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3 font-mono text-xs">components-reconnect-rejected</td><td class="p-3 text-muted-foreground">The server refused the session. Only a reload helps.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            Blazor also fills <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">#components-reconnect-current-attempt</code>,
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">#components-reconnect-max-retries</code> and
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">#components-seconds-to-next-attempt</code>.
            Each id may appear only once — the framework looks them up by id and takes the first.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Overriding it</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The markup is yours, so all four of these work:
        </p>
        <ul class="ms-4 list-disc space-y-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><strong class="text-foreground">Change nothing.</strong> Every colour comes from a theme variable, so it already matches your application in both modes.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-foreground">Add your own classes.</strong> The library's rules live in the <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">components</code> cascade layer, below your utilities, so your classes win without <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">!important</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-foreground">Write different markup.</strong> Any element with that id works; the block above is only a starting point.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-foreground">Point Blazor elsewhere.</strong> Set <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reconnectionOptions.dialogId</code> when you start Blazor and the library's CSS never matches.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    [Inject] private IJSRuntime JS { get; set; } = default!;

    private async Task Preview(string classes)
    {
        // The countdown ticks here the way the framework ticks it -- once a second -- so the
        // preview shows the real behaviour rather than a number frozen at 5.
        await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("eval", $$"""
            (() => {
                const m = document.getElementById('components-reconnect-modal');
                if (!m) return;
                clearInterval(window.__bbReconnectPreview);
                const set = (id, v) => { const el = document.getElementById(id); if (el) el.innerText = v; };
                set('components-reconnect-current-attempt', '12');
                set('components-reconnect-max-retries', '30');
                m.className = '{{classes}}';

                if (m.classList.contains('components-reconnect-retrying')) {
                    let secs = 5;
                    let attempt = 12;
                    set('components-seconds-to-next-attempt', secs);
                    window.__bbReconnectPreview = setInterval(() => {
                        secs -= 1;
                        if (secs < 1) { secs = 5; attempt += 1; set('components-reconnect-current-attempt', attempt); }
                        set('components-seconds-to-next-attempt', secs);
                    }, 1000);
                }

                setTimeout(() => {
                    clearInterval(window.__bbReconnectPreview);
                    m.className = '';
                }, 12000);
            })();
            """);
    }
}
